WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:10.100
The President of Colombia, Gustavo Petro,
came to power in 2022 with great promises

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:14.110
of change in practically all political, social
and economic sectors of the country.

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:17.880
You could say that the new president set out
to reform Colombia from top to bottom.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.109
One of these major changes is, precisely,
its energy transition plan, which, surprising

00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:26.830
as it may seem, involves an abrupt halt to
any new oil exploration and exploitation contracts

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:27.840
in Colombian territory.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.029
In other words, Colombia could become one
of the main oil powers to veto this industry.

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:33.460
If not the first of all.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:36.790
And, keep in mind we are talking about one
of the most important economic sectors, the

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:40.590
one that contributes the most money to the
public coffers and the top one in terms of

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:41.590
exports.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:45.340
The question is, what could all this mean
for Colombia to break with the oil industry?

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:46.340
Why are they doing it?

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:47.340
What are they going to sacrifice?

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.510
Does such a measure even make sense?

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.410
Well, this video will answer all these questions.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:01:05.610
Let's get started.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:10.180
With proven reserves of nearly 2 billion barrels,
Colombia is among the 35 countries with the

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.630
largest reserves of black gold in the world.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:16.750
Specifically, there are 23 geological zones
in this country, with various basins, of which

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:21.930
only nine produce hydrocarbons: seven produce
oil and two produce gas.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:27.789
Of the seven crude oil producing basins, only
three account for more than 90% of production.

00:01:27.789 --> 00:01:31.520
These are the Eastern Plains, Middle Valley
and Upper Magdalena Valley basins.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.780
In total, of the 20 departmental units that
currently extract oil, only one extracts light

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.979
oil, while the rest produce medium or heavy
crude.

00:01:38.979 --> 00:01:42.530
These would be roughly the details.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.869
As you can see, the Colombian oil profile
is not exactly that of a country like Saudi

00:01:45.869 --> 00:01:47.320
Arabia, but it does have potential.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.680
Although, not surprisingly, they had a hard
time making the most of it.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:56.700
Stay tuned because we’re going back to where
this whole story begins.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.710
(T1.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:05.299
THE FIRST STEPS: AN ALLERGY TO PROFITABILITY)

00:02:05.299 --> 00:02:09.460
The first oil well in the history of Colombia
was drilled in 1918, in the Cira-Infantas

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:12.130
field, where barely 2,000 barrels per day
were produced.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:14.950
Three years later, the country's first refinery
was built in Barrancabermeja and the first

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:17.980
oil pipeline was constructed in Mamonal, near
Cartagena.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:21.350
And this is how the Colombian oil industry
was launched more than 100 years ago.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:24.170
However, for a long time no profit was made
from this industry.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:29.410
In 1951, seeking to turn this situation around,
the government of Laureano Gómez nationalized

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:34.080
and granted several oil concessions to the
newly formed Empresa Colombiana de Petróleos,

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.080
Ecopetrol, a public company created so that
the Colombian State could actively participate

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:39.280
and promote this industry.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.000
And so, although giants of those years such
as Texaco and Shell began to take an interest

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.470
in Colombian oil, the exploration and extraction
conditions were totally disadvantageous for

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:48.470
them.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.610
The presence of Ecopetrol was the main stumbling
block.

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:54.300
The government only had eyes for it, and this
entity was not only a company that competed

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:59.200
in exploration and production, but also acted
as the public body that regulated this sector.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:02.890
So in one way or another, all private companies
were obliged to partner with their virtual

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:04.950
competitor on unattractive terms.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:09.250
Therefore, throughout almost the entire 20th
century, various legal regimes were tried

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:13.910
and tested, which, to tell the truth, seemed
to go from bad to worse.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:21.830
For example, between 1974 and 1989, a regime
called "50/50 Contract" operated, which had

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.250
the mission of promoting investment.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:27.110
However, under this framework, the private
partner assumed the cost and risk of the exploration

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:30.150
investment, which had to be completed within
a maximum period of three years,

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:33.330
However, when production began, the remaining
profit was distributed as follows:

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:38.090
20% for the State in royalties, 40% for Ecopetrol,
and 40% for the private investor.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:41.170
Of course, the private investor then had to
pay the corresponding profit taxes.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:42.170
The result?

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:45.300
Between 85% and 90% of the exploitation profits
went to the State.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:51.769
The private investor barely received the rest,
which, not surprisingly, was very unattractive.

00:03:51.769 --> 00:03:57.250
In 1987 an attempt was made to improve this
regulation, although attempt is a bit of an

00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:00.700
overstatement: the only thing that was changed
was that ECOPETROL took a higher participation

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:02.069
in the cost of exploration.

00:04:02.069 --> 00:04:04.390
But it did not change the model in general
terms.

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:08.790
Therefore, in 1989 a new regime was implemented,
which would remain in force until 1994.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:12.220
Then, the figure known as the "Production
Sharing Contract" was created.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:16.169
In this scheme, the private partner assumed
the exploratory cost and risk, until the discovery

00:04:16.169 --> 00:04:17.169
was made.

00:04:17.169 --> 00:04:21.709
At that time Ecopetrol would participate as
follows:

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:25.669
As you can see, the more profitable the discovered
well was, the lower the proportion of profits

00:04:25.669 --> 00:04:26.800
was left for the investor.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.870
If this model attracted a flood of investment,
it must have done so far from Colombian oil.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:35.060
Because, in fact, a field with barely 60 million
barrels was already scarcely profitable.

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:39.300
Of course, this model did not work, so in
1995 a new formula was launched, called the

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:40.300
"R FACTOR".

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:43.650
In this case, production distribution was
based on the profitability of the oil field

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.860
without taking into account the magnitude
of the reserves.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:49.860
However, it was not at all easy to calculate
the expected revenues and profits, which produced

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:50.860
a lot of uncertainty.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:53.820
An uncertainty that, not surprisingly, scared
off investors.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:58.639
So now I'm sure you understand why Colombia's
oil wealth was barely exploited during practically

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:00.050
the entire 20th century.

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:03.699
And the main interested party in the growth
of production was the State itself, which

00:05:03.699 --> 00:05:06.930
took most of the benefits in terms of taxes
and royalties.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:10.979
So I'm sure, I'm certain many of you have
the solution in your head, it seems pretty

00:05:10.979 --> 00:05:11.979
obvious.

00:05:11.979 --> 00:05:15.430
However, it took a long time arriving.

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:20.400
Listen up.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:25.729
TAKE-OFF)

00:05:25.729 --> 00:05:27.360
All was not lost.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.090
During Andrés Pastrana's presidency, ECOPETROL
made a very important move.

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:35.030
During what is known as the Round 2000 call,
the State made a series of tenders to explore

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:39.190
and produce in projects in which the state-owned
company would only have a 30% share.

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:41.990
Of course, this caught the attention of the
world's oil majors.

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:45.919
To give you an idea, this initiative culminated
in the awarding of 13 projects, with an initial

00:05:45.919 --> 00:05:48.270
investment of more than US$600 million.

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:52.870
However, the big change for the Colombian
oil sector was yet to come: In 2003, then

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:57.610
President Alvaro Uribe Velez ordered the restructuring
of the sector, going straight to the root

00:05:57.610 --> 00:05:58.610
of the problem.

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:02.680
The Colombian government of the time created
the National Hydrocarbons Agency, so that

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:04.270
Ecopetrol lost its regulatory powers.

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:08.669
And so, the public company would be completely
focused on exploration, production, transportation,

00:06:08.669 --> 00:06:10.860
refining and commercialization of hydrocarbons.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.610
In other words, it became an oil company in
the usual sense.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:17.789
At the same time, the government implemented
a new royalty, tax and fee contract.

00:06:17.789 --> 00:06:21.370
The new model mimicked international standards
and limited the State's total share to a range

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:23.360
of 50-60% of extracted profits.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:24.569
And do you know what happened?

00:06:24.569 --> 00:06:28.330
From that moment on, production skyrocketed,
and with it, so did exports.

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:31.599
Suddenly, the Colombian oil sector came to
life.

00:06:31.599 --> 00:06:34.750
Take a look.

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:38.840
And so, while Venezuelan production was falling,
Colombia was gaining more and more presence

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:40.320
in Latin America's oil sector.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:45.449
The production levels reached would have been
completely unimaginable just a few years earlier.

00:06:45.449 --> 00:06:48.540
Of course, there were also problems.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:53.560
The drop in oil prices in 2015 reduced investment
in this sector by about 30% and the drilling

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:55.690
of new wells by more than 80%.

00:06:55.690 --> 00:07:00.020
The government of Juan Manuel Santos then
needed to propose a tax reform to contain

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:02.759
the drop in tax revenues generated by oil.

00:07:02.759 --> 00:07:06.110
Let's just say that the Colombian treasury
had become very dependent on the new public

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:08.280
revenues brought in by the black gold.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:09.909
But, let's not get sidetracked.

00:07:09.909 --> 00:07:13.350
In spite of everything, the sector consolidated
and hydrocarbon exports continued to gain

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:15.050
strength among Colombian exports.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:19.640
Today this industry is responsible for slightly
more than one third of all Colombian exports.

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:23.540
In a way, we are talking about a kind of oil
miracle that contributed to changing the country.

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:27.919
In just a decade, between 2003 and 2013, the
country went from almost having to import

00:07:27.919 --> 00:07:31.919
crude oil to becoming a major supplier to
countries such as the United States.

00:07:31.919 --> 00:07:32.919
And do you know what?

00:07:32.919 --> 00:07:36.310
Right now, with the lack of investment that
for years has been happening in the international

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:40.620
oil industry and the Russian invasion of Ukraine
have brought forth the possibility of Colombia

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:43.750
experiencing a new golden era of hydrocarbons.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:46.090
However, the new president has said no way.

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:51.280
That oil exploitation has no place in Colombia.

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:55.419
Listen up.

00:07:55.419 --> 00:07:59.340
PETRO YES, "LEO" NO)

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:04.590
At the end of 2021, in the midst of Colombia's
presidential election campaign, the future

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:08.199
of the oil industry was a relevant topic on
the candidates' economic agenda.

00:08:08.199 --> 00:08:12.240
The most forceful position was that of Gustavo
Petro, who promised nothing less than the

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:14.139
cessation of oil concessions.

00:08:14.139 --> 00:08:23.380
Check it out.

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:37.330
Well, this candidate ultimately became the
president-elect of the Republic of Colombia

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:39.620
on 19 June 2022.

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:43.770
And in November of 2022, just one year after
his promise, he launched into an even more

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:47.120
daring speech at the UN Climate Change Conference.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:56.520
Check it out.

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:18.390
But wait, because the real bombshell came
on 19 January 2023, when Irene Velez, Minister

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:28.250
of Mines and Energy, confirmed that Bogota
will no longer sign new oil exploration contracts:

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:37.390
Naturally, this has generated a lot of fear,
not only in the oil sector, but in the entire

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:41.270
industry and practically the entire Colombian
economy: will this country lose one of its

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:43.750
most important sources of dollars and public
income?

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:46.840
Is security of supply at risk?

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:53.690
You see, Colombia currently holds 330 exploration
and exploitation contracts, which will continue

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:58.360
to produce hydrocarbons for both the domestic
market and for export for approximately ten

00:09:58.360 --> 00:09:59.360
years.

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:01.170
The question is, what happens after that?

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:03.500
Well, this is what President Petro says:

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:07.940
C1("According to International Energy Agency
projections, we have five years of coal exports

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:11.860
and 10 years of oil exports (...) The transition
has to do with two aspects.

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:16.170
One, to ensure that the foreign currency we
lose from coal and oil exports is replaced

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:20.230
to the same extent by exports of clean energy
and tourism, mainly.

00:10:20.230 --> 00:10:25.490
Two, (getting) our own internal consumption
of coal and oil to be replaced by electrifying

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:29.330
transportation, changing our energy matrix
and decarbonizing production."

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:32.820
– Gustavo Petro, 21 January 2023)

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:38.150
C2("There will be a strict energy transition,
but maintaining oil exports, at levels that

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:41.760
will allow us to have the foreign currency
the country needs (...) I would say that the

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:44.100
oil transition will last about 15 years".

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:48.860
– José Antonio Ocampo, Minister of Finance
and Public Credit of Colombia)

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:52.640
In other words, he seems to have more or less
a plan: to force the energy transition.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:54.570
The question is, will it be achieved in ten
years?

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:57.730
Is it worth losing billions of dollars of
potential revenue in a country that is still

00:10:57.730 --> 00:11:06.399
very poor and has so many shortcomings in
fundamental areas such as infrastructure?

00:11:06.399 --> 00:11:08.860
At the end of the day, the data are very clear.

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:10.500
They are compelling.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:14.321
C3("The hydrocarbon industry is fundamental
for the country's economic stability, national

00:11:14.321 --> 00:11:17.139
financing and that of the departments and
municipalities.

00:11:17.139 --> 00:11:23.730
Nearly 40% of exports depend on this sector,
20% of the Nation's fiscal income and 76%

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:24.850
of royalties.

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:29.279
Diversifying the export basket must be an
objective, but not at the cost of withering

00:11:29.279 --> 00:11:30.279
the industry."

00:11:30.279 --> 00:11:34.970
Communiqué of the Colombian Oil and Gas Association,
20 January 2023)

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:39.170
It seems VisualPolitik viewers, the controversy
and the fear of losing billions of dollars

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:42.520
in foreign investment and public revenues
was such that Petro himself seems to have

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:43.850
backtracked.

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:44.850
At least, partially.

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:48.490
Yes, he does continue to target the hydrocarbon
industry and possibly the granting of new

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:52.910
exploration and production licenses will plummet,
but at least the energy transition plan presented

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:54.730
includes new concessions.

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:59.020
Be that as it may, the future of this industry
in Colombia does not look particularly bright.

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:04.579
And the question is, is the backtracking by
President Petro enough?

00:12:04.579 --> 00:12:08.360
What impact could it have for Colombia if
this president fulfills his plans?

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:09.360
Well...

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:11.990
Stay tuned.

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:14.950
(T4.

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:20.320
FROM OIL MIRACLE TO OIL DEBACLE?)

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:24.240
It seems that words always count, but a president's
statements usually count much more.

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:28.160
With his rhetoric, Petro has burned the confidence
of a sector that was going full steam ahead.

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:32.440
In fact, in 2022, investment in exploration
reached its best record since 2014.

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:37.690
It is now expected that in 2023 the trend
will turn around and investment will fall

00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:38.690
again.

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:40.279
And we are talking about concessions that
have already been signed.

00:12:40.279 --> 00:12:44.440
And of course, if Petro's plans are consolidated,
then the problems could go far beyond the

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:48.120
oil companies, the inflow of foreign currency
into the country and even the government's

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:49.170
own revenues.

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:52.459
We are talking about thousands and thousands
of jobs, we are talking about the fact that

00:12:52.459 --> 00:12:57.020
fuel prices could become more expensive, which
would harm the income of millions of families.

00:12:57.020 --> 00:13:01.040
And that's not even the end of it.

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:05.870
The cessation of oil production in 10 to 15
years could also bring with it another major

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:08.540
problem: the questioning of the security of
energy supply.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:09.540
Why?

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:13.070
Because the Colombian electricity system is
highly dependent on hydroelectric generation.

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:17.140
To give you an idea, during normal hydrological
periods, hydroelectric generation can supply

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:19.380
more than three quarters of all demand.

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:22.760
However, when phenomena such as El Niño and
the resulting drought periods occur, then

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:24.329
the country has a problem.

00:13:24.329 --> 00:13:28.980
So far, hydrocarbons have solved the problem,
but if they run out, things could become very

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:29.980
complicated.

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:33.360
Be that as it may, as you can see, Colombia
seems willing to sacrifice tens of billions

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:36.190
of dollars and thousands of jobs to fight
global warming.

00:13:36.190 --> 00:13:37.190
And guess what?

00:13:37.190 --> 00:13:38.500
Now it’s your turn for some questions:

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:40.990
What do you think of Colombia's plan and do
you agree with it?

00:13:40.990 --> 00:13:44.470
Leave us your thoughts in the comments and
let's open a debate.

00:13:44.470 --> 00:13:49.050
And as always, if you found this video interesting,
don't forget to like and subscribe to VisualPolitik

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:50.050
if you haven't already done so.

00:13:50.050 --> 00:13:51.050
Thank you very much for watching,

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:52.899
All the best and see you next time.

